Description: glsnake: give both light positions a w component
 glLightfv(GL_POSITION) reads four floats, but light_pos held three per
 light. Light 0's w was light_pos[1][0], which is 0.0. Light 1's w came from
 past the array, from stack memory that gl_init() never sets: valgrind's
 memcheck reports "Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised
 value(s)" in the OpenGL driver, with the value "created by a stack
 allocation" in gl_init().
 .
 That stray w is the low half of a pointer, so light 1 moved from run to run.
 With address randomisation on, 25 frames of earlier runs showed 9 different
 pictures. With it off (setarch -R), 6 of 6 stock runs drew the same picture,
 but preloading a library that draws nothing (an OpenGL layer with its output
 disabled) still changed 5.07% of the pixels, because it changes what was on
 the stack before gl_init().
 .
 A large |w| puts light 1 at the viewer. That is the picture in 17 of the 25
 frames and in all 6 runs with setarch -R. The authors' (0, 20, -1) only takes
 effect when w is small, and w = 0 would make light 1 a directional light
 that 1 of the 25 frames came near. Write light 0 out as it reads today, with
 w = 0, and put light 1 at the viewer, (0, 0, 0, 1): it draws that usual
 picture pixel for pixel, in every run.
 .
 XScreenSaver 6.17 has the same arrays, and memcheck reports the same read
 there (glsnake.c:1414).
